<h2 id="quick-answer">What should a dad do during labor?</h2>
<p><strong>Be a calm, attentive support person. Know your partner’s preferences, handle the practical details, offer comfort she actually wants, and help her communicate with the care team. Follow her lead and the team’s guidance as labor changes. Your job is to support her, not direct the birth.</strong></p>
<p>A lot of expecting dads picture one big moment: the drive, the pushing, the first time they hold the baby. In between those moments, there can be waiting, uncertainty, repeated questions, and long stretches where being useful looks pretty ordinary.</p>
<p>You might refill a water cup when the team says she can drink. You might find the charger. You might say almost nothing because she has asked for quiet. None of that is a supporting role in the movie sense. It is the actual work.</p>
