<h2 id="before">Do the preparation while you can still think in full sentences</h2>
<p>Ask your partner what kind of support sounds good to her. Does she like touch when she is overwhelmed? Encouragement? Quiet? Who should receive updates, and who should wait until you are both ready? These are easier conversations on the couch than between contractions.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/birth-plan-template">birth plan template</a> to talk through preferences together. Keep the plan accessible, and understand that preferences can change. If she wants a different pain relief option than she expected, support the person in front of you.</p>
<p>Learn the basics in a childbirth class. Ask your care team what signs should prompt a call, where to go, and what to do if labor starts before the expected time. Save those instructions. There is no single contraction timing rule that is right for every pregnancy, distance from the hospital, or medical situation.</p>
<p>Take care of transport, parking, childcare for older kids, pet care, and your work contact. Check the hospital’s policy for support people. If a doula will be involved, talk about how you can work together. Knowing who handles what helps you stay focused on your partner.</p>
<h3>Pack your own bag</h3>
<p>Bring a charger with enough reach, comfortable clothes, toiletries, identification, and your own snacks and water. Check what paperwork and supplies your hospital requests. Keep your partner’s things easy to find, so “Can you get my lip balm?” does not become a full unpacking exercise.</p>
<p>Download the <a href="/hospital-bag-checklist">free hospital bag checklist</a> and review it together. The goal is a bag you can use, not a bag impressive enough to photograph.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Get the bag handled now" body="Use the free checklist, check your hospital\u2019s requirements, and put the essentials somewhere you can find them." href="/hospital-bag-checklist" label="Get the hospital bag checklist" />

<h2 id="during">During labor, offer one useful thing at a time</h2>
<p>Try a clear choice: “Would you like my hand, or some space?” If she wants touch, ask the nurse or midwife to show you a comfort technique suited to the situation. If she does not want it, stop. You do not need to take the change personally or explain that it helped ten minutes ago.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/preparing-for-labour-and-birth/tips-for-your-birthing-partner-or-partners/">NHS tips for birth partners</a> include reassurance, breathing together, and helping with position changes. Check with the care team about movement, eating, and drinking, especially when monitoring, medication, or procedures affect what is appropriate.</p>
<p>Pay attention between contractions, too. Does she want the room quieter? Is a family group chat lighting up the phone? Does she need the nurse? Small details are easier for you to notice because you are not the one doing the physical work of labor.</p>
<h3>What to say, and when to stop talking</h3>
<p>Keep your words short and believable. “I am here.” “Do you want me closer?” “I will ask the nurse.” You can be reassuring without predicting when the baby will arrive or promising that something will not hurt.</p>
<p>Avoid “calm down,” “you are almost done” when you do not know that, or an update about how uncomfortable your chair is. If she asks you to stop talking, that is useful information. Quiet presence counts.</p>
<h3>Be the person who manages the outside world</h3>
<p>Send only the updates you both agreed to share. Put the phone away afterward. Ask before taking photos or video, and check hospital rules. A moment can belong to your family without becoming content or a group text immediately.</p>
<p>Eat and drink enough to function. If you need a bathroom break, coordinate it with your partner and the team. If you feel faint, tell a staff member and sit down safely. Staying useful includes recognizing your own limits early.</p>
