<h2>Help her be heard without speaking over her</h2>
<p>Start by listening when a clinician explains something. If your partner wants help asking questions, you can say, “Could you explain that again in plain language?” or “She has a question before we move on.” Give her the first chance to answer questions about her own body and preferences.</p>
<p>When a decision comes up and time allows, useful questions include: What are you recommending? Why? What are the benefits and risks? Are there alternatives? How much time do we have to decide?</p>
<p>Those questions are for understanding the situation. They are not a script for delaying urgent care or arguing your way into the birth you pictured. If the team says action is urgent, listen and follow directions.</p>
<p>If she tells you something feels wrong or asks for help, take it seriously and alert the team. You can say exactly what she reported and when it began. Clear information is more helpful than trying to name the problem yourself.</p>
<h2 id="changes">If the plan changes, stay with the person</h2>
<p>An induction, an epidural, an assisted birth, or a C section can bring a new set of instructions and feelings. Your partner does not need a performance review of the original plan. Ask what she needs now.</p>
<p>If a C section is recommended, ask where you can be, what you should wear, and what happens next. If you are allowed beside her, focus on her face and keep your reassurance simple. If you must wait elsewhere, ask how you will receive updates and follow the team’s instructions.</p>
<p>If your baby needs separate care, ask the team to help you understand where everyone is going and how to keep your partner informed. Do not promise an outcome you cannot know. “I am finding out what happens next” is honest and useful.</p>
<p>Afterward, let her tell the story in her own way and at her own pace. A safe outcome can coexist with a frightening experience. If either of you is struggling with what happened, ask the care team about follow up support.</p>
