<h2 id="after">Your job keeps going after the baby arrives</h2>
<p>Ask what your partner needs before arranging visitors or announcements. Help her reach her belongings and call the nurse when she needs assistance. After surgery or other interventions, follow staff guidance about movement and recovery rather than trying to help her up on your own.</p>
<p>Ask someone to show you the baby care you are unsure about. Practice the diaper change. Learn the feeding plan together. Write down discharge instructions and ask who to contact for questions about her recovery and the baby’s health.</p>
<p>Before the first night, learn safe sleep basics: place your baby on their back in a separate infant sleep space with a firm, flat surface and a fitted sheet, without soft bedding or toys. The <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/reproductive-health/features/babies-sleep.html">CDC safe sleep guidance</a> explains the setup. If you are getting sleepy while holding the baby, put them in their safe sleep space or ask an awake caregiver for help.</p>
<p>A video monitor can help you keep an eye on that safe sleep space without hovering over the crib at every rustle.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Want eyes on the crib without hovering over it?" body="A baby monitor lets you check on your baby's safe sleep space from another room, so you are not standing over the crib all night." href="https://babymonitordirectllc.pxf.io/rEq1o3" label="See baby monitors" affiliate />

<p>For more preparation around the nights ahead, a structured sleep resource may be useful. Keep expectations appropriate to a newborn and follow the baby’s individual feeding advice.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Want a structured resource for baby sleep?" body="Explore Taking Cara Babies for guidance suited to your baby’s stage. Check the program’s age range and follow your clinician’s feeding and medical advice." href="https://www.takingcarababies.com?sca_ref=11694397.hvn2tySyLaykC" label="Explore Taking Cara Babies" affiliate />

<h2 id="checklist">The delivery room checklist worth remembering</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Know:</strong> her preferences, the care team’s instructions, and who to contact.</li><li><strong>Ask:</strong> whether she wants touch, quiet, encouragement, or help getting the nurse.</li><li><strong>Handle:</strong> the bag, transport, messages, and other practical details.</li><li><strong>Listen:</strong> to her first, and to the clinical guidance.</li><li><strong>Adapt:</strong> when her needs or the birth plan change.</li><li><strong>Stay involved:</strong> in recovery and baby care after the birth.</li></ul>
<p>For the relationship side of showing up, listen to <a href="/podcast#our-wives-get-honest-about-pregnancy-postpartum-us">the conversation with Vivian and Abigail</a>. For what comes next, read our <a href="/blog/first-time-dad-guide">first time dad guide</a>. And if you still have weeks or months to prepare, start with <a href="/blog/how-to-support-your-pregnant-wife">supporting your partner during pregnancy</a>.</p>
